00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:34.020
of like legging type workout things

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:35.220
that I will not be working out in

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:36.570
but I'm gonna wear them every day.

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:37.864
And t-shirts and a jacket.

00:00:37.864 --> 00:00:38.697
- Yeah. It's all you need.

00:00:38.697 --> 00:00:40.537
- And like two shoes in case one breaks.

00:00:40.537 --> 00:00:42.330
I'm like, I'm the worst.

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:44.190
I think its touring so much though.

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:45.840
I didn't wanna lug all
the (beep) everywhere.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:46.673
Are you not supposed to-

00:00:46.673 --> 00:00:48.300
I didn't wanna lug everything everywhere.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:49.303
- Right
- It's a lot of stuff.

00:00:49.303 --> 00:00:50.190
- It's a lot of stuff.

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:52.020
- I know. I'm keeping it daytime.

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:53.610
So, have y'all met before?

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:54.570
- No.
- Okay.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:55.403
- Never.

00:00:55.403 --> 00:00:56.550
- We just like to bring people together.

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:57.442
- I love that.
- Love it.

00:00:57.442 --> 00:00:59.010
- Okay.
- Strangers on the couch.

00:00:59.010 --> 00:00:59.850
- Strangers on a couch.

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:02.070
That was the name of our
initial thought for the show.

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:03.720
- [Macklemore] Yeah.
- Strangers on the couch.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:05.130
Um, no. We both have kids.

00:01:05.130 --> 00:01:06.617
We have two each, I
think, but you have three.

00:01:06.617 --> 00:01:07.450
- I have three.

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:08.769
- [Kelly] Three kiddos.
- Wow, how's that?

00:01:08.769 --> 00:01:10.530
- [Kelly] That's a lot.
You're outnumbered.

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:12.600
- We're playing zone defense, a lot.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:15.000
And it's the best.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:16.830
- Oh my God.
- And then it's the worst.

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:17.970
And then it goes back to the best.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:19.170
And you just keep going.

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:20.250
Until you get through breakfast.

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:22.380
- That is actual parenting,
you should write a book.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:23.340
That's exactly what it-

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:24.437
Oh my gosh.

00:01:24.437 --> 00:01:26.451
- Cheeks
- So delicious.

00:01:26.451 --> 00:01:28.224
Oh, I could smile for days.

00:01:28.224 --> 00:01:30.060
So what do your kids think of your music?

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:31.320
Do you play your stuff?

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:32.940
Because sometimes I can't play mine.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:34.260
'Cause cussing.
- Yeah.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.483
No, my kids cuss like sailors.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:38.370
They're dad raps.

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:39.300
It's just gonna happen.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:40.299
It just happens.

00:01:40.299 --> 00:01:41.237
- I tell mine they can't.

00:01:41.237 --> 00:01:42.070
- There's things that I'm really good.

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:43.436
- Until they pay their bills.
- There you go.

00:01:43.436 --> 00:01:44.269
- Yeah.
- That's smart.

00:01:44.269 --> 00:01:45.420
- Do they think you're cool?

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:46.663
- Uh...

00:01:46.663 --> 00:01:48.420
- My kids don't think I'm cool.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:49.762
And I keep thinking to myself like,

00:01:49.762 --> 00:01:52.440
you're gonna grow up one
day and think I'm so cool.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:53.273
- That's what I think too.

00:01:53.273 --> 00:01:54.993
- Like, I know cool people.

00:01:54.993 --> 00:01:55.826
(Kelly laughing)

00:01:55.826 --> 00:01:56.970
I hang out with cool people.

00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:58.230
- [Kelly] How do you not get this?

00:01:58.230 --> 00:01:59.460
- Yeah, "How do you not understand?"

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:00.570
- I think our kids don't, yeah.

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:01.650
They're around us all the time.

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:02.483
I don't know.

00:02:02.483 --> 00:02:06.419
- Yeah, there's moments
of pivotal career times

00:02:06.419 --> 00:02:09.150
in my life where like I'll look at my kids

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:11.269
and be like, "See what your dad just did?"

00:02:11.269 --> 00:02:12.102
(Kelly laughing)

00:02:12.102 --> 00:02:12.997
- "Do you appreciate this?"
- They don't care.

00:02:12.997 --> 00:02:13.830
- "I'm incredible."

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:14.760
- "'Cause I just killed
it. Most of the time?

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:16.127
Not at all, but just
then it was a moment."

00:02:16.127 --> 00:02:18.570
- "This one time, I'm amazing."

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:19.672
- [Macklemore] Yes.

00:02:19.672 --> 00:02:20.505
(guests and audience laughing)

00:02:20.505 --> 00:02:23.220
- You've been a really open
about relapsing, right?

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.890
So you, you relapsed during COVID.

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:26.792
- Yeah.
- Is that-

00:02:26.792 --> 00:02:28.320
What do you think?

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:29.760
I mean, that was a hard
time for everybody.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:31.860
Emotionally, physically, financially.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:34.050
Like it kind of hit everybody.

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:36.150
Either all of that or, or you
know, a little bit of that.

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:38.643
What do you think hit you
to where you relapsed?

00:02:39.643 --> 00:02:42.090
(Macklemore sighs)

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:44.129
My heart started beating when
you started talking about it.

00:02:44.129 --> 00:02:48.810
It was an intense time.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:51.210
You know, the life that I knew,

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:53.970
just like all of our
lives, was stripped away.

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:56.970
I'm used to a certain schedule
of touring, of being gone.

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:57.803
Of being home.

00:02:57.803 --> 00:03:00.540
Of recovery and being able to go

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:02.640
to a physical 12-step meeting.

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:04.680
And that stopped during COVID

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.770
and eventually the Zooms weren't-

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:09.690
I was on Instagram while being on Zoom

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:11.551
and I'm just not really paying
attention to the meetings.

00:03:11.551 --> 00:03:15.690
And eventually I thought,
and this is what happens

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:17.670
to me if I don't prioritize my recovery

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:19.110
if I don't put that first.

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:19.943
- Yeah.

00:03:19.943 --> 00:03:21.659
- Then I will lose
everything in front of it.

00:03:21.659 --> 00:03:22.492
- Yeah.

00:03:22.492 --> 00:03:23.325
- That I'm putting in front of it.

00:03:23.325 --> 00:03:24.990
And that's what happened.

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:27.780
And luckily it was only a couple weeks

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:30.232
but it caused a lot of pain.

00:03:30.232 --> 00:03:31.345
You know, still.

00:03:31.345 --> 00:03:34.260
I still feel that pain

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:36.030
and it's been two and a half years

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:38.312
I'm still working of getting back trust.

00:03:38.312 --> 00:03:43.312
But I think that it gave me a
reprieve of a reminder, again.

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:45.630
A slap in the face.

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:49.170
Not a delicate reminder, but
a slap in the face reminder,

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:50.460
this is your life.

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:51.990
You get one of these.

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:53.160
What are you going to do

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:55.050
with your precious time on this earth?

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:56.580
Do you want to be secretive?

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:57.480
Do you want to be hiding?

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:00.510
Do you want to be in the shadows

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:03.570
or do you want to live your
life to the fullest potential?

00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:04.590
Get back on it.

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:05.694
And be honest.

00:04:05.694 --> 00:04:06.750
(audience clapping)

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:09.750
- I think the most important thing

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:14.640
that you just said for
me is those meetings.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:17.610
That being present and
being held accountable

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:19.290
and like on a, you know-
- Community.

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:20.580
- Weekly thing, and
like having a community.

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:21.413
- Community, yes

00:04:21.413 --> 00:04:22.980
- Having that support is so important.

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:25.680
And that's everybody-

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:26.550
I mean, not everybody,

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:27.840
but I know a lot of people relapsed

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:28.980
in their own addictions,

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:30.600
their own things they had going on in life

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:32.670
because they weren't, they
didn't have any support.

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:35.850
We realized how fragile
we are without community,

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:36.750
without our village.

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:39.322
Like, without our people that understand

00:04:39.322 --> 00:04:41.040
and even just showing up

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:42.840
and having a regular routine of something.

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:44.100
Like, that's hard.

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:46.050
I think we all realize
that in our own lives.

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:46.883
- Right.

00:04:46.883 --> 00:04:48.390
- So before the break,

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:50.550
we were talking about
your relapse during COVID.

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:53.400
So how do you think that
impacted or affected your album?

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:56.490
- I think that pain is a
catalyst for great art.

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:58.530
I don't want to inflict the pain

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:00.060
on myself anymore to make art.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:02.400
You know, it's not like
I need to self sabotage

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:03.360
in order to create.

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:07.530
But I think that it created
some darker, more honest,

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:09.000
vulnerable moments on the album.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:11.319
And I think that that's important.

00:05:11.319 --> 00:05:13.980
Again, a relapse is not, you know,

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:15.330
it's not why I relapsed

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:17.610
or any sort of part of the process,

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:20.793
but it does create that vulnerability.

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:23.160
So I think that that's probably the effect

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:25.320
that it had on the album
was just real pain.

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:26.220
- Yeah.

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:29.250
Which that's why, I don't
know like why you both got

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:33.210
into the art we're in, but
like, that's my outlet.

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:34.770
Like it became my outlet when I was a kid.

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:35.790
Like, I started writing

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:37.050
and it was like more poetry

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:38.700
before I like started singing or whatever.

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:40.740
But that was like, that's how I like-

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:42.000
People are always like, "Oh man, why-"

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:44.077
'Cause sometimes people are
always like and I say this too,

00:05:44.077 --> 00:05:47.670
"I write better when I'm
more in a sad or hard place."

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:48.503
- Right.

00:05:48.503 --> 00:05:49.336
- Like, it's harder.

00:05:49.336 --> 00:05:51.180
Like, 'cause I'm not really
writing to get the happy out.

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:52.140
I like to keep the happy in.

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:53.310
- Yeah.
- It's like I'm trying to-

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:54.875
Did you get into it maybe for an outlet?

00:05:54.875 --> 00:05:55.980
Was it your outlet?
- Absolutely.

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:56.915
It still is my outlet.

00:05:56.915 --> 00:05:58.320
- Me too.
- It still is my therapist.

00:05:58.320 --> 00:05:59.460
On top of my therapist, but-

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:00.903
- Me too!

00:06:00.903 --> 00:06:05.580
- I also think that most
artists have like an enormous

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:08.430
amount of empathy and
no one ever teaches us

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:09.900
how to deal with that empathy.

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:10.733
- Yes.

00:06:10.733 --> 00:06:13.230
- Nobody teaches us that
it doesn't need to pierce-

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:15.210
- It's overwhelming.
- Yeah.

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:18.126
- No one teaches us how
to not be overwhelmed

00:06:18.126 --> 00:06:22.230
by the hardships we see
in life or face in life.

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:25.050
So I think that's a reason

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:27.240
why a lot of artists self-medicate.

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:28.073
- [Macklemore] Yes.

00:06:28.073 --> 00:06:29.880
- 'Cause nobody's ever taught them

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:33.660
how to protect themselves while creating.

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:34.590
And while feeling.

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:35.423
- Yeah.

00:06:35.423 --> 00:06:37.050
Well, because the process of creation

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:38.550
is to strip away these layers, right?

00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:40.620
We're peeling away
these layers constantly.

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:42.030
Let me get closer to the heart.

00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:43.410
Let me get closer to the heart.

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:44.678
Let me try to get inside.

00:06:44.678 --> 00:06:48.420
And once we do that, then
we become more vulnerable.

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:51.720
We're like susceptible
to feeling that empathy

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:54.420
or that compassion or that
pain or whatever it is.

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:57.120
And, yeah, we're not taught the tools.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:06:58.470
- Yeah. Not at all.

00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:00.420
I hear that you found
a new passion though.

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:01.350
Golf?
- [Macklemore] Yes.

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:03.120
- You're a golfer.
- Yes.

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:06.210
- I feel like you probably
look fabulous on a golf course.

